---
title: "Architecture"
description: "The architecture of Outpost for self-hosted deployments."
---

![Outpost Architecture](/outpost-images/architecture.png)

Outpost consists of three services that can be run as a single process or as separate deployments for independent scaling.

- **API Service** — Captures events and exposes the APIs for configuring tenants and destinations.
- **Delivery Service** — Delivers events to tenant destinations. Contains adapters for each destination type. Requires a configured message queue (SQS, RabbitMQ, GCP Pub/Sub, Azure Service Bus, or NATS JetStream).
- **Log Service** — Enables storing and retrieving events, their status, and delivery responses. Requires PostgreSQL or ClickHouse.

Running Outpost as individual services is optional. You can run it as a single binary if horizontal scaling of individual services isn't needed for your event volume.

## MCP Events

[MCP Events](/docs/outpost/guides/mcp-events) adds work to each service:

- **API Service** handles the MCP endpoints. It sends callback verification challenges and `terminated` envelopes to callback URLs on the internet, so it needs outbound HTTPS, like the Delivery Service. It also runs the MCP subscriptions worker below.
- **Delivery Service** delivers events to `mcp` destinations, with the [MCP delivery rules](/docs/outpost/destinations/mcp#delivery-rules).
- **Log Service** keeps each subscription's delivery status in Redis as it records attempts, for the `deliveryStatus` a refresh returns.

Only the API Service loads topic schemas and applies them at startup, after migrations. When the schemas change, it checks the change against the last applied ones and fails to start on a [breaking change](/docs/outpost/features/topics#schema-evolution-and-deprecation) to a topic with live subscriptions.

### MCP subscriptions worker

The `mcp-subscriptions` worker runs in every API Service instance. Every `MCP_EXPIRY_SWEEP_INTERVAL` (30 seconds by default), one instance at a time, holding a Redis lock, runs a pass that:

- Deletes subscriptions that expired longer ago than twice `MCP_EXPIRY_SWEEP_INTERVAL`, between 5 and 60 seconds (60 seconds by default), and emits [`mcp.subscription.expired`](/docs/outpost/features/operator-events) for each. Delivery already stops at `expires_at`.
- Ends subscriptions to topics that are no longer MCP-enabled, and those left from a breaking schema change, and sends each a `terminated` envelope. It only does so when the instance runs the last applied topic schemas, so instances on an older or rolled-back configuration never end subscriptions. Once no instance has reported the last applied schemas for 60 seconds, as when a rollback outlives them, the instance applies its own.

Each instance also records the topic configuration it runs, every 20 seconds, so a rolling deploy can restart instances on the previous configuration without failing the breaking-change check.

The worker logs failures and retries on the next pass, so it never fails `/healthz`.

## Runtime Requirements

**API Service & Delivery Service:**
- Redis 6.0+ or wire-compatible alternative (RDB or AOF persistence strongly recommended)
- One of the supported message queues: Azure Service Bus, AWS SQS, GCP Pub/Sub, RabbitMQ, or NATS JetStream

**Log Service:**
- PostgreSQL or ClickHouse

For the tables and keys Outpost writes to each of these stores, and how they relate, see [Data Model](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/data-model).
